Essentially you must create a program that can plot a function that is in the form z = f(x,y) using JavaScript and the Three.js library. 

Your program must generate input values for x and y between -1 and 1 in increments of 0.1 and use this to plot x, y, and z vertices to be included as part of a mesh that will then be graphically displayed. You must implement the functionality to allow both the function to be mapped as a mathematical formula and the input range values to be entered by the user of your program.

Your program must implement mouse controls (such as OrbitControls) such that the function that you have mapped can be rotated and scaled using the mouse.

Your project MUST include a plane and an axis helper to provide a point of reference for your function that indicates the origin which is assumed to be x=0, y=0, and z=0. The axis helper should indicate the direction of x, y, and z within the scene.

Your function grapher should be able to accommodate any function that can be expressed and evaluated as a mathematical function. For the assignment, please ensure the program can graph the following specific examples:
- f(x, y) = Math.sqrt(x*x + y*y) (which produces a cone shape)
- f(x, y) = x*x - y*y (which produces a hyperbolic paraboloid)

NOTE: if you implement the functionality to allow the user to enter any function that they want then these specific examples are not required to be hardcoded so long as your program can graph them.

The input values can be generated using simple for loops as indicated below:

```javascript
for (let x = -1; x <= 1; x += 0.1) {
  for (let y = -1; y <= 1; y += 0.1) {
    let z = fun(x, y);
    // capture vertices using new THREE.Vector3(x, y, z)
  }
}
```

Keep in mind that you must capture all of the vertices that you are defining and provide them as input into one of the geometry functions within Three.js. The ParametricGeometry object is perhaps the best one to use, however, if your research identifies another approach like BufferGeometry feel free to use it so long as you can achieve the same results in Three.js.

This is a complete implementation using HTML5, JavaScript, and Three.js. 

I have used **`BufferGeometry`** to construct the mesh. This is the modern standard in Three.js for creating custom shapes. To handle the math, I use a `Function` constructor which allows the program to evaluate the user's string input as a mathematical expression.

### The Implementation

You can save this code as an `.html` file (e.g., `grapher.html`) and open it in any modern web browser.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>3D Function Grapher - Three.js</title>
    <style>
        body { margin: 0; overflow: hidden; font-family: sans-serif; }
        #ui {
            position: absolute;
            top: 20px;
            left: 20px;
            background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.9);
            padding: 15px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            z-index: 100;
            box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.3);
            max-width: 300px;
        }
        .input-group { margin-bottom: 10px; }
        label { display: block; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold; }
        input { width: 100%; padding: 5px; box-sizing: border-box; }
        button { 
            width: 100%; 
            padding: 10px; 
            background: #28a745; 
            color: white; 
            border: none; 
            cursor: pointer; 
            border-radius: 4px;
        }
        button:hover { background: #218838; }
        .hint { font-size: 11px; color: #666; margin-top: 5px; }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div id="ui">
        <div class="input-group">
            <label>Function f(x, y)</label>
            <input id="formula" type="text" value="Math.sqrt(x*x + y*y)">
            <div class="hint">Try: <code>x*x - y*y</code> or <code>Math.sin(x*5) * Math.cos(y*5) * 0.2</code></div>
        </div>
        <div class="input-group">
            <label>Range (Min / Max)</label>
            <div style="display: flex; gap: 5px;">
                <input id="minRange" type="number" value="-1" step="0.1">
                <input id="maxRange" type="number" value="1" step="0.1">
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="input-group">
            <label>Step Increment</label>
            <input id="step" type="number" value="0.1" step="0.01" min="0.01">
        </div>
        <button id="plotBtn">Graph Function</button>
    </div>

    <!-- Load Three.js and OrbitControls via CDN -->
    <script type="importmap">
        {
            "imports": {
                "three": "https://unpkg.com/three@0.160.0/build/three.module.js",
                "three/addons/": "https://unpkg.com/three@0.160.0/examples/jsm/"
            }
        }
    </script>

    <script type="module">
        import * as THREE from 'three';
        import { OrbitControls } from 'three/addons/controls/OrbitControls.js';

        // --- Configuration & Scene Setup ---
        const scene = new THREE.Scene();
        scene.background = new THREE.Color(0x222222);

        const camera = new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(75, window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight, 0.1, 1000);
        camera.position.set(2, 2, 2);

        const renderer = new THREE.WebGLRenderer({ antialias: true });
        renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
        document.body.appendChild(renderer.domElement);

        const controls = new OrbitControls(camera, renderer.domElement);

        // --- Helpers ---
        // Axis Helper: Red (X), Green (Y), Blue (Z)
        const axesHelper = new THREE.AxesHelper(2);
        scene.add(axesHelper);

        // Plane Helper (The floor/grid)
        const gridHelper = new THREE.GridHelper(2, 20, 0x444444, 0x888888);
        scene.add(gridHelper);

        // Light
        const light = new THREE.DirectionalLight(0xffffff, 1);
        light.position.set(5, 10, 7);
        scene.add(light);
        scene.add(new THREE.AmbientLight(0x404040));

        let currentMesh = null;

        // --- Logic to Plot the Function ---
        function plot() {
            const formulaStr = document.getElementById('formula').value;
            const min = parseFloat(document.getElementById('minRange').value);
            const max = parseFloat(document.getElementById('maxRange').value);
            const step = parseFloat(document.getElementById('step').value);

            // 1. Remove previous mesh
            if (currentMesh) {
                scene.remove(currentMesh);
                currentMesh.geometry.dispose();
                currentMesh.material.dispose();
            }

            // 2. Create the math function safely
            let mathFunc;
            try {
                mathFunc = new Function('x', 'y', `return ${formulaStr};`);
            } catch (e) {
                alert("Invalid Formula! Use JavaScript Math syntax (e.g., Math.sin(x))");
                return;
            }

            // 3. Generate Vertices and Indices
            const vertices = [];
            const indices = [];

            // We use a nested loop to create a grid of points
            // Note: In Three.js, Y is typically 'up'. 
            // We map Math x -> Three X, Math y -> Three Z, Math z (result) -> Three Y

            let rowCount = 0;
            let colCount = 0;

            for (let x = min; x <= max + 0.001; x += step) {
                colCount = 0;
                for (let y = min; y <= max + 0.001; y += step) {
                    let z;
                    try {
                        z = mathFunc(x, y);
                    } catch(e) { z = 0; }

                    // x, height(z), y
                    vertices.push(x, z, y);
                    colCount++;
                }
                rowCount++;
            }

            // 4. Generate Indices (Triangles)
            for (let i = 0; i < rowCount - 1; i++) {
                for (let j = 0; j < colCount - 1; j++) {
                    const a = i * colCount + j;
                    const b = i * colCount + (j + 1);
                    const c = (i + 1) * colCount + j;
                    const d = (i + 1) * colCount + (j + 1);

                    // Two triangles per grid square
                    indices.push(a, b, d);
                    indices.push(a, d, c);
                }
            }

            // 5. Build the Geometry
            const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();
            geometry.setIndex(indices);
            geometry.setAttribute('position', new THREE.Float32BufferAttribute(vertices, 3));
            geometry.computeVertexNormals(); // For smooth lighting

            const material = new THREE.MeshNormalMaterial({ 
                side: THREE.DoubleSide, 
                wireframe: false,
                flatShading: false 
            });

            currentMesh = new THREE.Mesh(geometry, material);
            scene.add(currentMesh);
        }

        // --- Event Listeners ---
        document.getElementById('plotBtn').addEventListener('click', plot);

        window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
            camera.aspect = window.innerWidth / window.innerHeight;
            camera.updateProjectionMatrix();
            renderer.setSize(window.innerWidth, window.innerHeight);
        });

        // --- Animation Loop ---
        function animate() {
            requestAnimationFrame(animate);
            controls.update();
            renderer.render(scene, camera);
        }

        // Initial Plot
        plot();
        animate();
    </script>
</body>
</html>
```

### Key Features of this Program:

1.  **Mathematical Flexibility**: It uses `new Function('x', 'y', 'return ' + formulaStr)` to evaluate user input. This means you can type any valid JavaScript math, such as:
    *   **Cone**: `Math.sqrt(x*x + y*y)`
    *   **Hyperbolic Paraboloid**: `x*x - y*y`
    *   **Waves**: `Math.sin(x*5) * Math.cos(y*5) * 0.2`
2.  **User Inputs**:
    *   **Formula**: Text input for $f(x, y)$.
    *   **Range**: Two numeric inputs for the Minimum and Maximum bounds of both $x$ and $y$.
    *   **Step**: Controls the resolution of the mesh. A smaller step (like 0.05) creates a smoother surface, while a larger step (0.2) is more blocky.
3.  **Visual Helpers**:
    *   **AxesHelper**: Shows the origin (0,0,0). Red is X, Green is Y (height in this visualizer), and Blue is Z.
    *   **GridHelper**: Provides a floor plane at $y=0$ for spatial reference.
4.  **BufferGeometry**: Instead of deprecated objects, this uses the modern `BufferGeometry` approach, manually calculating vertices and triangle indices based on the nested loops.
5.  **OrbitControls**: You can left-click to rotate, right-click to pan, and scroll to zoom in/out.
6.  **Responsive**: The canvas automatically resizes if you change your browser window size.

### How to use:
1. Copy the code into a file named `index.html`.
2. Open it in a browser.
3. To see the **Cone**, leave the default values and click "Graph Function".
4. To see the **Hyperbolic Paraboloid**, change the formula to `x*x - y*y` and click "Graph Function".

Joining multiple nested collections in MongoDB requires the **advanced `$lookup` syntax**, which allows you to define a sub-pipeline. This approach is essential when you need to join based on multiple conditions or when you need to perform joins on data already nested within arrays.

Here is a comprehensive guide using a real-world scenario: **Orders -> Products -> Categories.**

---

### 1. The Data Schema
Let's assume we have three collections:
1.  **`orders`**: Contains an array of items, each with a `productId`.
2.  **`products`**: Contains product details and a `categoryId`.
3.  **`categories`**: Contains category names and metadata.

---

### 2. The Complex Aggregation Example
This query performs the following:
1.  Filters orders.
2.  Unwinds the nested items array.
3.  Joins `products` using **multiple conditions** (ID match AND stock status).
4.  Joins `categories` nested inside the product result.
5.  Sorts and limits the final output.

```javascript
db.orders.aggregate([
  // 1. Optimization: Filter orders as early as possible
  { $match: { status: "completed" } },

  // 2. Handle nested array: Flatten the items so we can join each one
  { $unwind: "$items" },

  // 3. Advanced $lookup with multiple conditions
  {
    $lookup: {
      from: "products",
      let: { 
        order_prod_id: "$items.productId", 
        min_qty: "$items.qty" 
      },
      pipeline: [
        {
          $match: {
            $expr: {
              $and: [
                { $eq: ["$_id", "$$order_prod_id"] }, // Condition 1: ID Match
                { $gt: ["$stock_level", 0] }          // Condition 2: Custom logic
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        // 4. Nested Join: Join categories INSIDE the product pipeline
        {
          $lookup: {
            from: "categories",
            localField: "categoryId",
            foreignField: "_id",
            as: "category_details"
          }
        },
        { $unwind: "$category_details" }
      ],
      as: "product_info"
    }
  },

  // 5. Clean up: Remove empty joins (if product was out of stock)
  { $unwind: "$product_info" },

  // 6. Final processing stages
  { $sort: { "product_info.price": -1 } },
  { $limit: 10 },

  // 7. Optional: Project the final shape
  {
    $project: {
      customer: 1,
      productName: "$product_info.name",
      category: "$product_info.category_details.name",
      quantity: "$items.qty"
    }
  }
])
```

---

### 3. Key Mechanisms Explained

#### A. The `let` and `pipeline` syntax
Standard `$lookup` only allows a single field equality. By using `let`, you define variables from the **source** document (prefixed with `$$`). The `pipeline` then allows you to use `$match` with `$expr`, enabling complex logic like `$and`, `$or`, and comparisons between local and foreign fields.

#### B. Handling Nested Arrays (`$unwind`)
If your join key is inside an array (like `items.productId`), you must `$unwind` the array before the `$lookup`. This creates a separate document for every array element, allowing the join to occur for each item individually.

#### C. Recursive/Nested Lookups
You can place a `$lookup` **inside** the `pipeline` of another `$lookup`. This is highly efficient for hierarchical data (e.g., Order -> Product -> Category) because it keeps the data structure organized and reduces the number of documents passed through the main pipeline.

---

### 4. Query Optimization Guidance

Joining in MongoDB can be resource-intensive. Follow these rules to maintain performance:

1.  **Index the Foreign Field:** The most common cause of slow lookups is a lack of indexes. In the example above, `products._id` and `categories._id` **must** be indexed. If joining on a custom field, ensure that field has an index.
2.  **Filter Early (`$match`):** Always place `$match` at the very beginning of the pipeline. This reduces the number of documents that need to be processed by the `$lookup`.
3.  **Limit Data Volume (`$project`):** Inside the `$lookup` sub-pipeline, use `$project` to return only the fields you actually need. Don't carry bulky "description" or "image" fields through the join if they aren't required.
4.  **Avoid Large Unwinds:** If an array has thousands of elements, `$unwind` will create thousands of documents in memory. In such cases, consider using `$map` with `$lookup` (available in MongoDB 5.0+) or restructuring your data.
5.  **Use `$limit` before `$lookup` if possible:** If you only need the top 10 orders, sort and limit them **before** you perform the joins to avoid unnecessary processing for the other 1,000 orders.

### 5. Incorporating Other Stages

*   **`$addFields`**: Use this after a `$lookup` if you want to perform calculations (e.g., `totalPrice: { $multiply: ["$items.qty", "$product_info.price"] }`).
*   **`$group`**: If you `$unwind` at the start to perform the join, you often need to `$group` at the end to reconstruct the original order document with its now-enriched array of items.
*   **`$facet`**: Use this if you need to generate multiple aggregations (like a count and a list) from the same joined data set in a single query.

To solve this problem, we need to find the number of subarrays where the Bitwise XOR of all elements has an odd number of set bits.

### Key Insights

1.  **Parity of Set Bits and XOR:**
    Let $count(X)$ be the number of set bits (1s) in the binary representation of $X$. A fundamental property of XOR is:
    $count(X \oplus Y) \equiv count(X) + count(Y) \pmod 2$.
    This means the parity of set bits in the XOR sum of two numbers is the XOR of the parities of their individual set bits.

2.  **Prefix XOR Sums:**
    Let $P_k$ be the prefix XOR sum of the first $k$ elements: $P_k = A[1] \oplus A[2] \oplus \dots \oplus A[k]$, with $P_0 = 0$.
    The XOR sum of a subarray $A[i \dots j]$ is $P_j \oplus P_{i-1}$.

3.  **Condition for Odd Set Bits:**
    We want $count(P_j \oplus P_{i-1})$ to be odd. Based on the property from point 1:
    $count(P_j \oplus P_{i-1}) \text{ is odd} \iff (count(P_j) + count(P_{i-1})) \text{ is odd}$.
    This occurs if and only if one of $count(P_j)$ or $count(P_{i-1})$ is even and the other is odd.

### Algorithm

1.  Initialize `even_parity_count = 1` (for $P_0 = 0$, which has 0 set bits, an even number) and `odd_parity_count = 0`.
2.  Maintain a running XOR sum `current_xor`.
3.  Iterate through the array $A$:
    *   Update `current_xor ^= A[i]`.
    *   Calculate the number of set bits in `current_xor`.
    *   If the number of set bits is odd, increment `odd_parity_count`.
    *   If the number of set bits is even, increment `even_parity_count`.
4.  The total number of subarrays with an odd number of set bits in their XOR sum is the product of the number of prefixes with even parity and the number of prefixes with odd parity:
    `Result = even_parity_count * odd_parity_count`.

### Implementation

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

using namespace std;

/**
 * Function to count the number of set bits (1s) in an integer.
 * Built-in functions like __builtin_popcount are faster for this.
 */
int countSetBits(int n) {
    return __builtin_popcount(n);
}

void solve() {
    int N;
    if (!(cin >> N)) return;

    long long even_parity_count = 1; // P_0 = 0 has 0 set bits (even)
    long long odd_parity_count = 0;
    int current_xor = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        int a;
        cin >> a;
        current_xor ^= a;

        // Check parity of set bits in the current prefix XOR
        if (countSetBits(current_xor) % 2 != 0) {
            odd_parity_count++;
        } else {
            even_parity_count++;
        }
    }

    // Each pair of (even_parity_prefix, odd_parity_prefix) forms a valid subarray
    long long result = even_parity_count * odd_parity_count;
    cout << result << endl;
}

int main() {
    // Optimize I/O operations
    ios_base::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(NULL);

    int T;
    if (!(cin >> T)) return 0;
    while (T--) {
        solve();
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### Complexity Analysis

*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ per test case. We iterate through the array once and perform constant-time bit manipulation and counting. Since the sum of $N$ over all test cases is $10^6$, this easily fits within the time limit.
*   **Space Complexity:** $O(1)$ extra space (excluding input storage), as we only maintain a few counters and the current prefix XOR sum.

